/** Generated from the drums textbook. Re-run tools/import-drums-textbook.mjs. Do not edit by hand. */
export default {"id":"drums-level-9","number":9,"title":"Drum and Bass Engineering","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the drums textbook","summary":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:","modules":[{"id":"drums-m9-1","code":"9.1","title":"Drum & Bass Engineering — The DnB Metric Clock & Off-Beat Shuffle","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-9-1","title":"Section 9.1 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Drum &amp; Bass (DnB)</strong> is electronic music's high-speed rhythmic science. Operating at a relentless <strong>174.00–175.00 BPM</strong>, DnB combines high-velocity linear backbeats with syncopated breakbeat shuffles. At 174 BPM, a 16th note elapses in only <strong>86.2 ms</strong>, demanding absolute transient discipline, surgical low-end phase management, and precise metric scaffolding. Grounded in the <em>Drum and Bass Production Knowledgebase</em> and <em>DNB Cheat Sheet</em>, this module establishes the metric physics, tempo conventions, and compositional methodologies of Drum &amp; Bass."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Analyze</strong> the temporal mechanics of the 174 BPM metric clock.","<strong>Program</strong> the signature DnB syncopated kick/snare pattern (Kick on 1 &amp; 11; Snare on 5 &amp; 13).","<strong>Differentiate</strong> <strong>Method A (Break-Based Sampling)</strong> from <strong>Method B (Kit-First Synthesis)</strong>.","<strong>Construct</strong> 4-bar and 8-bar dancefloor scaffolding engineered for seamless club DJ mixing.","<strong>Implement</strong> 174 BPM Halftime metric structures.","<strong>Classify</strong> the primary DnB subgenres: Jungle, Liquid Funk, Jump-Up, Neurofunk, and Techstep."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-1-1","title":"9.1.1: Tempo Conventions: 160–180 vs. 174–175","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.1: Tempo Conventions: 160–180 vs. 174–175","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                           DRUM &amp; BASS TEMPO SPECTRUM &amp; CONVENTIONS\n==================================================================================================\nTEMPO BAND           GENRE CONTEXT                        PRODUCTION CHARACTERISTICS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n160 – 168 BPM        Early Jungle &amp; 1990s Breakbeat Rave  Looser funk shuffle; pitched vinyl breaks\n170 – 174 BPM        Modern Club Standard / Liquid / Rollers Optimal balance of groove and velocity\n174 – 175 BPM        Modern Dancefloor / Neurofunk / Jump-Up THE UNIVERSAL DEFAULT FOR NEW SETS\n176 – 180 BPM        Hardcore / Crossbreed / Fast Techstep Extreme kinetic speed; razor-tight transients\n==================================================================================================</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Studio Standard:</em> All new Drum &amp; Bass Ableton Live project templates must be initialized to <strong>174.00 BPM</strong> (or 175.00 BPM)."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-1-2","title":"9.1.2: The Off-Beat Shuffle and Step Coordinates","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.2: The Off-Beat Shuffle and Step Coordinates","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Unlike 4-on-the-floor house music, Drum &amp; Bass utilizes a <strong>syncopated linear backbeat</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                           THE STANDARD DNB 16-STEP MATRIX (174 BPM)\n==================================================================================================\nSTEP INDEX:     01    02    03    04    05    06    07    08    09    10    11    12    13    14    15    16\nMETRIC COUNT:   1     e     &amp;     a     2     e     &amp;     a     3     e     &amp;     a     4     e     &amp;     a\nKICK (Pad C1): [K]    .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .    [K]    .     .     .     .     .\nSNARE (Pad D1): .     .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .     .     .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .\nCLOSED HAT:    [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]   [H]\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Key Step Coordinates:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Kick 1:</strong> Step 1 (Beat 1.1) — Downbeat anchor.","<strong>Snare 1:</strong> Step 5 (Beat 2.1) — Primary first backbeat.","<strong>Kick 2 (The Syncopation):</strong> <strong>Step 11 (Beat 3.3)</strong> — Placed on the offbeat sixteenth note, creating the signature DnB forward stumble.","<strong>Snare 2:</strong> Step 13 (Beat 4.1) — Primary second backbeat."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-1-3","title":"9.1.3: Method A (Break-Based) vs. Method B","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.3: Method A (Break-Based) vs. Method B","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Grounded in local <em>DnB Knowledgebase</em> (§5.1), there are two distinct engineering methods to construct DnB drums:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  METHOD A (BREAK-BASED) vs. METHOD B (KIT-FIRST)        |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ METHOD A: BREAK-BASED SAMPLING ]                                     |\n|  • Slices classic funk breaks (Amen, Think, Hot Pants) into Drum Rack   |\n|  • Inherent vinyl warmth, natural room acoustics, human shuffle         |\n|  • Dominates: Jungle, Liquid Funk, Drumfunk, Ragga                      |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ METHOD B: KIT-FIRST SYNTHESIS / ONE-SHOTS ]                          |\n|  • Places pristine, pre-clipped synthetic one-shots on exact grid steps |\n|  • Maximum transient punch, surgical EQ separation, massive loudness   |\n|  • Dominates: Jump-Up, Dancefloor, Neurofunk, Techstep                  |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ METHOD C: THE MODERN HYBRID (The Professional Standard) ]            |\n|  • High-passed Breakbeat (Tops/Shuffle) layered over Method B Kick/Snare|\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-1-4","title":"9.1.4: Dancefloor Scaffolding in Bars of 4","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.4: Dancefloor Scaffolding in Bars of 4","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Club Drum &amp; Bass is arranged strictly around <strong>binary phrasing</strong> to facilitate DJ mixing:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                        DNB DANCEFLOOR STRUCTURAL SCAFFOLDING\n==================================================================================================\n4-BAR CELL:     [ Bar 1: Foundation ] [ Bar 2: Response ] [ Bar 3: Drive ] [ Bar 4: Turnaround Fill ]\nPHRASE BLOCK:   4 Cells = 16 Bars (Standard Intro or Drop A)\nDROP BLOCK:     8 Cells = 32 Bars (Full Club Dancefloor Drop)\n==================================================================================================</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"DJs mix on 32-bar and 64-bar phrases. A drum pattern that changes its fundamental kick/snare coordinates across an odd number of bars (e.g., 3 bars or 5 bars) will trainwreck in a live club DJ set."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-1-5","title":"9.1.5: Halftime Inside a 174 BPM Set","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.5: Halftime Inside a 174 BPM Set","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Halftime Drum &amp; Bass maintains the 174.00 BPM clock but shifts the snare from Steps 5 and 13 to <strong>Step 9 (Beat 3)</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["Perceived pulse drops to <strong>87.00 BPM</strong>.","Provides instant energy contrast during breakdowns or mid-song switches without requiring tempo automation."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-1-6","title":"9.1.6: Subgenre Listening Map","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.1.6: Subgenre Listening Map","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                           DNB SUBGENRE AUDITORY BENCHMARKS\n==================================================================================================\nSUBGENRE         PRIMARY METHOD      DRUM TIMBRE &amp; CHARACTER\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. Jungle        Method A (Breaks)   Chopped Amen break edits, reverse cymbals, fast rolls\n2. Liquid Funk   Method C (Hybrid)   Smooth rolling shakers, acoustic snare layers, open space\n3. Jump-Up       Method B (Kit-First)Huge punchy kick, snappy 200 Hz snare, minimal tops\n4. Neurofunk     Method B / Hybrid   Ultra-tight, gated, compressed, clinical precision\n5. Techstep      Method B (Kit-First)Cold, mechanical, sparse, industrial rimshots\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 9.1: The 174 BPM Core Pattern","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Create a new Ableton Live 12 Set at <code>174.00 BPM</code>.","Load your <strong>Master Hybrid Drum Rack</strong> onto Track 1.","Program the standard 1-bar DnB pattern:","Kick (Pad C1): Step 1 (Vel: 120) and Step 11 (Vel: 110).","Snare (Pad D1): Step 5 (Vel: 125) and Step 13 (Vel: 125).","Closed Hat (Pad F#1): Continuous 16th notes using dynamic velocity alternation (110, 65, 95, 60...).","Duplicate to 4 bars. On Bar 4 Step 15–16, program a fast double-snare fill.","Verify that the pattern drives with relentless kinetic momentum."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>What are the exact 16-step grid coordinates for a classic Drum &amp; Bass kick drum?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>Step 1</strong> (Beat 1.1) and <strong>Step 11</strong> (Beat 3.3).","<strong>What is the difference between DnB Method A and Method B drum construction?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Method A chops sampled acoustic funk breaks for natural swing and vinyl texture; Method B places discrete synthetic one-shot samples on rigid grid coordinates for maximum punch and loudness.","<strong>What is the universal master tempo default for modern Drum &amp; Bass production sets?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>174.00 BPM</strong> (or 175.00 BPM)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m9-2","code":"9.2","title":"Drum & Bass Engineering — Break Science & Slicing","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes The breakbeat is the acoustic DNA of Drum & Bass. Rather than constructing rhythms exclusively from synthetic drum machines, DnB was forged by sampling, time-stretching, and resequencing 1960s and 1970s ","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-9-2","title":"Section 9.2 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The breakbeat is the acoustic DNA of Drum &amp; Bass. Rather than constructing rhythms exclusively from synthetic drum machines, DnB was forged by sampling, time-stretching, and resequencing 1960s and 1970s funk and soul drum solos (\"breaks\"). Grounded in <em>Drum Breaks: A Guide To Injecting Character into Your Music</em> and the <em>Drum and Bass Production Knowledgebase</em>, this module analyzes the five legendary breaks, demonstrates non-destructive transient warping, and establishes the modern <strong>Hybrid Dual-Bus Routing Architecture</strong> (layering clean synthetic punch beneath dirty vintage vinyl breaks)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Differentiate</strong> between an acoustic drum break and a digital breakbeat.","<strong>Identify</strong> and categorize the five legendary breaks: <strong>Amen</strong>, <strong>Think</strong>, <strong>Apache</strong>, <strong>Hot Pants</strong>, and <strong>Funky Drummer</strong>.","<strong>Warp</strong> vintage acoustic drum recordings to 174.00 BPM without transient smearing using Live's <strong>Beats Mode</strong>.","<strong>Slice</strong> breakbeats directly into Ableton Live 12 <strong>Drum Racks</strong> (strictly avoiding prohibited Suite Sampler).","<strong>Resequence</strong> sliced audio transients into modern 174 BPM rolling patterns.","<strong>Implement</strong> the <strong>Punch Bus / Break Bus</strong> frequency-split layering architecture."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-2-1","title":"9.2.1: Break vs. Breakbeat: Acoustic Origins","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.1: Break vs. Breakbeat: Acoustic Origins","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>A Drum Break:</strong> A solo drum section in a 1960s/70s funk, soul, or jazz recording where all melodic instruments drop out, leaving only the acoustic drummer playing a continuous groove.","<strong>A Breakbeat:</strong> A digital audio loop created by sampling a drum break, chopping it into discrete transient slices, and re-sequencing those slices into a new syncopated electronic rhythm at high tempo (160–180 BPM)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-2-2","title":"9.2.2: Sampling Ethics and Modern Clean Workflows","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.2: Sampling Ethics and Modern Clean Workflows","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Vintage vinyl breaks exist in a complex legal copyright landscape. For commercial production:"},{"type":"list","items":["Use <strong>Royalty-Free High-Resolution Re-recordings</strong> or properly cleared sample packs.","When practicing break science, study the original vinyl breaks to internalize the micro-timing and ghost-note placement of legendary funk drummers."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-2-3","title":"9.2.3 – 9.2.7: The Five Legendary Breaks of Drum & Bass","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.3 – 9.2.7: The Five Legendary Breaks of Drum & Bass","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                        THE FIVE ESSENTIAL DRUM &amp; BASS BREAKS\n==================================================================================================\nBREAK NAME       ORIGINAL RECORDING (ARTIST / YEAR / DRUMMER)      ACOUSTIC CHARACTERISTICS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. Amen Break    The Winstons – \"Amen, Brother\" (1969)             Aggressive, dirty snare crack,\n                 Drummer: Gregory Sylvester \"G.C.\" Coleman         iconic 4-bar ghost-note syncopation.\n                 Original Tempo: ~136 BPM -&gt; Pitched to 174 BPM    THE FOUNDATION OF JUNGLE &amp; DNB.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2. Think Break   Lyn Collins – \"Think (About It)\" (1972)           Continuous high-energy tambourine\n                 Drummer: John \"Jabo\" Starks (James Brown Band)    shuffle, vocal squeaks (\"Yeah! Woo!\").\n                 Original Tempo: ~112 BPM -&gt; Pitched to 174 BPM    PRIMARY SHUFFLE LAYER IN LIQUID.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n3. Apache Break  Incredible Bongo Band – \"Apache\" (1973)           High-pitched bongo polyrhythms,\n                 Drummer: Jim Gordon / Perc: King Errisson         driving acoustic snare, bright cymbals.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n4. Hot Pants     Bobby Byrd – \"Hot Pants – I'm Coming\" (1971)      Heavy acoustic hi-hat sizzle,\n                 Drummer: John \"Jabo\" Starks                       punchy low-mid snare, tight funk.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n5. Funky Drummer James Brown – \"Funky Drummer\" (1970)              World's most famous ghost-snare roll,\n                 Drummer: Clyde Stubblefield                       tight open hi-hat dynamic wave.\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-2-8","title":"9.2.8: Warping Breakbeats in Live 12","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.8: Warping Breakbeats in Live 12","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"When loading an acoustic break recorded at 112–136 BPM into a 174.00 BPM project:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ Raw Breakbeat (.WAV) ] ---&gt; Ableton Clip View\n                                    |\n                                    +--&gt; Warp: [ ON ]\n                                    +--&gt; Mode: [ BEATS ]\n                                    +--&gt; Preserve: [ TRANSIENTS ]\n                                    +--&gt; Loop Mode: [ One-Way (-&gt;) ]\n                                    +--&gt; Transient Envelope: [ 55 - 75 ]</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["Lowering <strong>Transient Envelope</strong> to 60 applies an instantaneous decay envelope to each slice, eliminating vinyl surface hiss and room ring between hits while preserving razor-sharp transient attacks at 174 BPM."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-2-9","title":"9.2.9: Slicing to Drum Rack","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.9: Slicing to Drum Rack","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Studio Mandate:</em> All break slicing must be executed directly into <strong>Drum Rack</strong> using native <strong>Simpler</strong> instances. Loading breaks into Suite Sampler is prohibited."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ Warped Breakbeat Audio Clip ] ---&gt; Right-Click ---&gt; \"Slice to New MIDI Track\"\n                                                             |\n                                                             +--&gt; Slicing Preset: [ Built-in 0-Velocity ]\n                                                             +--&gt; Create Slices At: [ Transients ]\n                                                             |\n                                                             v\n[ Instant Drum Rack Created ] &lt;--- Slices mapped chromatically: C1, C#1, D1, D#1...</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-2-10","title":"9.2.10: Resequencing 2 Bars into Modern 174 DnB","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.10: Resequencing 2 Bars into Modern 174 DnB","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Once sliced into Drum Rack, resequence the original funk slices into the modern DnB pattern:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 RESEQUENCING THE AMEN BREAK INTO MODERN DNB (174 BPM)\n==================================================================================================\nSTEP INDEX:     01    02    03    04    05    06    07    08    09    10    11    12    13    14    15    16\nMETRIC COUNT:   1     e     &amp;     a     2     e     &amp;     a     3     e     &amp;     a     4     e     &amp;     a\nAMEN SLICE:    [KIK1]  .    [HAT]  .   [SNR1]  .    [GHT]  .    [HAT]  .   [KIK2]  .   [SNR2] [GHT][GHT] .\nPAD NUMBER:     Pad 1       Pad 4       Pad 3       Pad 6       Pad 4       Pad 2       Pad 3  Pad 6 Pad 6\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-2-11","title":"9.2.11 & 9.2.12: The Hybrid Dual-Bus Routing Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.2.11 & 9.2.12: The Hybrid Dual-Bus Routing Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A raw 1970s funk break lacks the low-end sub impact (50 Hz) and transient click (4 kHz) required for modern sound systems. We solve this via <strong>Frequency-Split Dual Bus Layering</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  THE HYBRID DUAL-BUS ROUTING ARCHITECTURE               |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ BUS A: THE DIRTY BREAK BUS (Method A) ]                              |\n|  • Sliced Amen / Think Break in Drum Rack                               |\n|  • EQ Eight: High-Pass Filter @ 350 Hz (Strips old acoustic sub/kick)   |\n|  • Saturator: Gentle analog clip (+2 dB)                                |\n|  • Role: Delivers 100% of the vintage vinyl shuffle, ghost notes &amp; air |\n|                                                                         |\n|  [ BUS B: THE PUNCH BUS (Method B) ]                                    |\n|  • Modern Synthetic Kick (DS Kick) + Modern Synthetic Snare (DS Snare)  |\n|  • EQ Eight: Clean sub fundamental @ 90 Hz + Snare snap @ 200 Hz        |\n|  • Role: Delivers 100% of the physical chest punch and low-end weight   |\n|                                                                         |\n|  SUMMED TO MASTER DRUM GROUP: Cohesive, Massive Modern DnB Rhythm       |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 9.2: Break Slicing & Dual-Bus Layering Workshop","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Load the <strong>Amen Break</strong> (<code>Amen_136.wav</code>) into an audio track in Live 12. Set project tempo to <code>174.00 BPM</code>.","Engage <strong>Warp → Beats Mode</strong> (Preserve Transients, Envelope: 65).","Right-click and execute <strong>Slice to Drum Rack</strong>.","On the sliced Drum Rack track, insert <strong>EQ Eight</strong> and high-pass all frequencies below 350 Hz.","On a parallel track, program a modern synthetic DS Kick (Steps 1 &amp; 11) and DS Snare (Steps 5 &amp; 13).","Sum both tracks into a <strong>Drum Group Bus</strong> hosting <strong>Glue Compressor</strong>. A/B test against the raw break to observe the massive increase in punch and clarity."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Who was the drummer that performed the legendary \"Amen Break\" in 1969?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>Gregory Sylvester \"G.C.\" Coleman</strong> (The Winstons – \"Amen, Brother\").","<strong>Why must a sliced acoustic breakbeat be high-passed at ~350 Hz when layering under a modern synthetic kick and snare?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> To eliminate the thin, muddy, out-of-phase low-frequency acoustic kick and low-mid resonance from the original vinyl recording, allowing the modern synthetic kick and snare to own the sub and punch bands cleanly.","<strong>What is the signature sonic element that makes the \"Think Break\" (Lyn Collins, 1972) instantly recognizable?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Its high-energy acoustic tambourine shuffle and vocal squeak/shout samples (\"Yeah! Woo!\")."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m9-3","code":"9.3","title":"Drum & Bass Engineering — Jungle Chopping & Ragga Syncopation","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-9-3","title":"Section 9.3 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Jungle</strong> is the direct precursor and foundational pillar of Drum &amp; Bass. Emerging from early 1990s UK sound system culture, Jungle treats chopped funk breakbeats not merely as background rhythm, but as the <strong>primary lead instrument</strong>. Jungle drum programming is characterized by frenetic micro-edits, 32nd-note stutters, reverse-cymbal chips, rapid pitch glides, and heavy Jamaican sound system (Ragga/Dub) syncopation. Grounded in the <em>Drum and Bass Production Knowledgebase</em> and historical breakbeat literature, this module teaches the art of intricate break manipulation."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Treat</strong> chopped breakbeats as melodic lead instruments with dynamic pitch, envelope, and panning variation.","<strong>Execute</strong> 32nd-note ratchet stutters, micro-edits, and reverse slice chips in Ableton Live 12.","<strong>Program</strong> Ragga/Dub offbeat syncopations, timbale rolls, and acoustic rimshot accents.","<strong>Establish</strong> historical tempo authenticity (160–168 BPM classic vs. 170–175 BPM modern Jungle).","<strong>Construct</strong> an authentic, evolving 16-bar Jungle drum sketch."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-3-1","title":"9.3.1: Chopped Breaks as the Lead Instrument","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.1: Chopped Breaks as the Lead Instrument","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In standard electronic music, drums maintain a static loop while synths provide melody. In <strong>Jungle, the drums ARE the lead</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  THE JUNGLE DRUM PHILOSOPHY                             |\n|                                                                         |\n|  Standard Electronic Track: [ Static Drum Loop ] + [ Lead Synth Melody ]|\n|                                                                         |\n|  Authentic Jungle Track:    [ Sliced Amen Break ]                       |\n|                             • Pitch Transposition (+/- 12 semitones)    |\n|                             • Micro-Stutters &amp; Polyrhythmic Chops       |\n|                             • Acts as the primary melodic vocal/hook    |\n|                                    +                                    |\n|                             [ Rolling 808 Sine Sub-Bass Bed ]           |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-3-2","title":"9.3.2: Slicing Anatomy: Stutters, Micro-Chops, and Reverse Chips","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.2: Slicing Anatomy: Stutters, Micro-Chops, and Reverse Chips","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                        JUNGLE MICRO-EDIT CHOPPING TECHNIQUES\n==================================================================================================\nTECHNIQUE             MIDI / AUDIO IMPLEMENTATION                 ACOUSTIC EFFECT\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. 32nd Stutter       Repeat a 1/32 slice 4 times at rising vel   Machine-gun snare roll into downbeat\n2. Reverse Chip       Reverse a 50 ms cymbal/snare tail slice     Instant vacuum-suck transition\n3. Pitch Glide        Pitch-shift a snare slice up +7 semitones   Creates frantic, rising kinetic tension\n4. Ghost Scramble     Rapidly alternate ghost slices (Steps 7-10) Frenetic breakbeat shuffle\n==================================================================================================</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 THE JUNGLE MICRO-CHOP MATRIX (BAR 4 TURNAROUND)\n==================================================================================================\nSTEP INDEX:     01    02    03    04    05    06    07    08    09    10    11    12    13    14    15    16\nMETRIC COUNT:   1     e     &amp;     a     2     e     &amp;     a     3     e     &amp;     a     4     e     &amp;     a\nAMEN SLICE:    [KIK]  .    [HAT] [REV] [SNR]  .    [STU][STU][STU][STU][KIK]  .   [SNR] [P+5][P+7][P+12]\nACTION:        Kick        Hat   Rev   Snare       32nd Snare Stutter  Kick        Snare Pitch Ascent!\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-3-3","title":"9.3.3: Ragga and Dub Sound System Syncopation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.3: Ragga and Dub Sound System Syncopation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Jungle inherits its rhythmic swing from Jamaican sound system culture (Dub, Reggae, Dancehall):"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Rimshot Accents:</strong> Placing tuned acoustic rimshots on unexpected upbeats (e.g., Step 4, Step 7).","<strong>Timbale Rolls:</strong> Pitch-swept high-frequency toms executing fast triplet fills across phrase boundaries.","<strong>Space &amp; Dub Echo:</strong> Dropping out all drums on Bar 8 Step 1, leaving only an isolated snare hit echoing through an auxiliary tape delay."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-3-4","title":"9.3.4: Historical Tempo Truth: 160–168 vs. Modern 174","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.4: Historical Tempo Truth: 160–168 vs. Modern 174","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                             JUNGLE TEMPO AUTHENTICITY MAP\n==================================================================================================\nERA                     TEMPO BAND        PRODUCTION DISTINCTIONS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nClassic Jungle (1993–1996) 160 – 168 BPM  Deep organic swing; loose funk feel; Akai S950 time-stretch\nModern Jungle (2020s+)     170 – 175 BPM  High-speed club tempo; integrates with modern DnB sets\n==================================================================================================</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Rule:</em> Teach Jungle tempo as a historical range. When producing classic vintage jungle, target <strong>164.00–168.00 BPM</strong>; when building modern dancefloor jungle, target <strong>174.00 BPM</strong>."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-3-5","title":"9.3.5: The 16-Bar Jungle Drum Sketch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.3.5: The 16-Bar Jungle Drum Sketch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A complete Jungle drum track is arranged in 16-bar evolving blocks:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Bars 1–4:</strong> Core Amen roll with basic kick/snare foundation.","<strong>Bars 5–8:</strong> Introduce Think Break tambourine shuffle and subtle ghost chops.","<strong>Bars 9–12:</strong> High-energy chopped variations; pitch-shifted snare accents.","<strong>Bars 13–16:</strong> Maximum frantic stutter edits; Bar 16 ends in a complete 1-bar drum drop."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 9.3: 16-Bar Jungle Breakbeat Construction","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Ableton Live 12 tempo to <code>168.00 BPM</code>.","Slice the <strong>Amen Break</strong> to a Drum Rack on Track 1.","Program an evolving 16-bar Jungle arrangement:","Apply 32nd-note stutters on Bar 4 and Bar 8 turnarounds.","Insert reversed cymbal slices on the upbeat preceding Bar 9.","Apply pitch transpositions (± 5 st) to snare slices in Bar 15.","Insert <strong>Saturator</strong> (Soft Clip) and <strong>EQ Eight</strong> on the Drum Group.","Verify that the drums function as a dynamic, melodic lead line."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Why is the drum track considered the \"lead instrument\" in classic Jungle production?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Jungle treats chopped breakbeat slices as melodic hooks, manipulating their pitch, duration, reverse playback, and rhythmic micro-edits to drive the primary musical interest.","<strong>What historical tempo band defined classic 1990s UK Jungle?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>160.00 to 168.00 BPM</strong>.","<strong>What is a \"Reverse Chip\" in breakbeat editing?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> A micro-slice (30–80 ms) of a cymbal or snare decay tail played in reverse immediately preceding a downbeat to create a dynamic vacuum-suck effect."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m9-4","code":"9.4","title":"Drum & Bass Engineering — Liquid Funk & Hypnotic Rollers","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-9-4","title":"Section 9.4 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Liquid Funk</strong> (pioneered by artists such as Calibre, LTJ Bukem, High Contrast, and Logistics) represents the soulful, musical dimension of Drum &amp; Bass. Unlike the aggressive distortion of Neurofunk or the frantic edits of Jungle, Liquid drums are engineered for <strong>smooth kinetic propulsion (\"the roll\")</strong>, organic acoustic warmth, and spectral transparency. This module details the construction of Liquid drum tracks, the deployment of the <strong>Think Break</strong> tambourine shuffle, and the hypnotic arrangement principles of modern <strong>Rollers</strong>."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Engineer</strong> clean, organic drum kits utilizing acoustic snare layers, warm kicks, and ride cymbals.","<strong>Carve</strong> frequency pockets (200–500 Hz and 1–3 kHz) within the drum bus to accommodate rich Rhodes pianos, lush synth pads, and vocal stems.","<strong>Deploy</strong> the <strong>Think Break</strong> and <strong>Hot Pants</strong> breaks as high-frequency rolling shuffle layers.","<strong>Differentiate</strong> between soulful Liquid Funk and hypnotic minimal Rollers.","<strong>Construct</strong> an evolving 16-bar Liquid drum arrangement at 174.00 BPM."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-4-1","title":"9.4.1: Clean, Musical Drum Acoustics","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.4.1: Clean, Musical Drum Acoustics","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Liquid Funk, drum hits must sound natural, warm, and acoustically believable:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  LIQUID FUNK DRUM ACOUSTIC CRITERIA                     |\n|                                                                         |\n|  • Kick Drum      : Warm analog/acoustic fundamental @ 85-95 Hz         |\n|                     Zero harsh digital click; rounded transient         |\n|  • Snare Drum     : Organic wooden body (200-220 Hz) + acoustic wire ring|\n|                     Never hard-clipped; gentle glue compression         |\n|  • Hi-Hats/Rides  : Multi-sampled acoustic cymbals; wide stereo air     |\n|  • Shaker Engine  : Continuous 16th-note organic shaker bed (Think Break)|\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-4-2","title":"9.4.2: Rolling but Gentle: Carving Space for Harmony","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.4.2: Rolling but Gentle: Carving Space for Harmony","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Liquid tracks feature dense musical arrangements (Fender Rhodes pianos, warm minor-ninth string pads, vocal hooks). If the drum bus is overly thick in the low-mids, the mix collapses into unresolvable mud."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 LIQUID DRUM BUS SPECTRAL CARVING (EQ EIGHT)\n==================================================================================================\nFREQUENCY BAND      ACTION               MUSICAL BENEFIT\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n250 Hz – 400 Hz     Surgical -3 dB Cut   Clears low-mid space for lush Rhodes chords and vocal warmth\n800 Hz – 1.5 kHz    Gentle -2 dB Dip     Clears mid-range space for vocal intelligibility\n10 kHz Shelf        Smooth +1.5 dB Boost Adds silky, high-end \"air\" to acoustic ride cymbals\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-4-3","title":"9.4.3: Liquid Funk vs. Modern Liquid","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.4.3: Liquid Funk vs. Modern Liquid","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Liquid Funk (Historical):</strong> Soulful, disco/funk-infused, heavily reliant on Think/Hot Pants acoustic breaks, live jazz basslines, and brass stabs (170–174 BPM).","<strong>Modern Liquid (Contemporary):</strong> Ultra-clean synthetic Method B foundation paired with pristine, high-passed acoustic tops, cinematic piano chords, and deep sub-rollers."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-4-4","title":"9.4.4: Rollers: Hypnotic Kinetic Momentum","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.4.4: Rollers: Hypnotic Kinetic Momentum","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A <strong>Roller</strong> is a Drum &amp; Bass track designed around a continuous, hypnotic, forward-driving groove that prioritizes groove consistency over dramatic drops."},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                     THE HYPNOTIC ROLLER 16-STEP MATRIX (174 BPM)\n==================================================================================================\nSTEP INDEX:     01    02    03    04    05    06    07    08    09    10    11    12    13    14    15    16\nMETRIC COUNT:   1     e     &amp;     a     2     e     &amp;     a     3     e     &amp;     a     4     e     &amp;     a\nKICK (Warm):   [K]    .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .    [K]    .     .     .     .     .\nSNARE (Acoust): .     .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .     .     .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .\nTHINK SHUFFLE: [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]   [T]\nRIDE CYMBAL:   [R]    .     .     .    [R]    .     .     .    [R]    .     .     .    [R]    .     .     .\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"The Engine of the Roll:","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The momentum of a Roller is driven by the <strong>Think Break tambourine</strong> running continuously on all sixteen steps with alternating velocity waves (110, 60, 90, 55...), glued together by a shared room reverb."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-4-5","title":"9.4.5: The 16-Bar Liquid Drum Sketch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.4.5: The 16-Bar Liquid Drum Sketch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                      16-BAR LIQUID DRUM ARRANGEMENT\n==================================================================================================\nBARS 1–4:   Kick + Snare + Subtle Shaker (Sparse, peaceful foundation)\nBARS 5–8:   Think Break shuffle layers in (High-passed @ 400 Hz); ghost snares active\nBARS 9–12:  Acoustic Ride Cymbal enters on quarter notes; stereo width expands\nBARS 13–16: Full dynamic energy; Bar 16 features a smooth acoustic tom roll into next phrase\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 9.4: Liquid Funk Drum Construction","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Ableton Live 12 to <code>174.00 BPM</code>.","Load a warm acoustic kick (Pad C1), an acoustic wooden snare (Pad D1), and the <strong>Think Break</strong> (high-passed @ 400 Hz).","Program the 16-bar Liquid arrangement detailed in Lesson 9.3.5.","Insert <strong>Glue Compressor</strong> on the drum group:","Attack: 30 ms | Release: Auto | Ratio: 2:1 | Gain Reduction: 2 dB.","Insert <strong>EQ Eight</strong> and cut -3 dB at 320 Hz.","Add a Rhodes electric piano track on Track 2 and confirm that the chords and drum groove sit in flawless acoustic harmony."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Which legendary drum break provides the signature high-frequency tambourine shuffle in Liquid Funk?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> The <strong>Think Break</strong> (Lyn Collins – \"Think (About It)\", 1972).","<strong>Why must the low-mid frequency band (250–400 Hz) be carved on a Liquid Drum &amp; Bass bus?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> To clear acoustic space for complex harmonic instruments (Rhodes pianos, vocal harmonies, lush synth pads) that occupy that identical spectral region.","<strong>What is the primary aesthetic priority of a Drum &amp; Bass \"Roller\"?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Maintaining a continuous, hypnotic, rolling kinetic momentum and groove consistency rather than relying on abrasive sound design or dramatic build-up stops."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m9-5","code":"9.5","title":"Drum & Bass Engineering — Jump-Up & Dancefloor Impact Scaffolding","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-9-5","title":"Section 9.5 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Jump-Up</strong> and <strong>Modern Dancefloor Drum &amp; Bass</strong> (Sub Focus, Dimension, Hedex, Chase &amp; Status) dominate festival mainstages and club sound systems. Unlike break-heavy subgenres, Jump-Up and Dancefloor rely exclusively on <strong>Method B (Kit-First Synthesis / Hard One-Shots)</strong>: large, punchy kicks, massive 200 Hz clipped snares, stripped-back hi-hats, and rigid binary scaffolding engineered specifically for DJ double-drops and maximum club impact."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Design</strong> high-impact synthetic kicks and 200 Hz snares using Method B engineering.","<strong>Execute</strong> hard clipping and transient limiting on the dancefloor drum bus without dynamic smearing.","<strong>Construct</strong> 16-bar and 32-bar dancefloor phrasing scaffolding aligned with professional DJ mixing standards.","<strong>Program</strong> high-visibility transition fills that club DJs can anticipate across phrase boundaries.","<strong>Construct</strong> an authentic 16-bar Jump-Up/Dancefloor drum sketch at 175.00 BPM."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-5-1","title":"9.5.1: Big, Simple, Punchy Drum Design","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.5.1: Big, Simple, Punchy Drum Design","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Dancefloor and Jump-Up DnB, the drum track must deliver maximum physical punch while remaining acoustically uncluttered to leave room for massive mid-range basslines:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  METHOD B DANCEFLOOR ACOUSTIC PROFILE                   |\n|                                                                         |\n|  • KICK DRUM (Pad C1):                                                  |\n|    - Fundamental @ 95 Hz - 110 Hz (Sits cleanly ABOVE sub-bass)         |\n|    - Hard transient click @ 4.5 kHz                                     |\n|    - Gated Decay: 70 ms (Ultra-tight; zero sub rumble)                  |\n|                                                                         |\n|  • MAIN SNARE (Pad D1):                                                 |\n|    - Heavy body fundamental @ 200 Hz - 220 Hz                           |\n|    - Hard-clipped composite with wide stereo noise transient            |\n|    - Total Decay: 140 ms                                                |\n|                                                                         |\n|  • HI-HATS &amp; TOPS:                                                      |\n|    - Stripped back: Closed 16th hats or offbeat 8ths only               |\n|    - Zero vinyl hiss or background break clutter                        |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-5-2","title":"9.5.2: Method B Construction in Drum Rack","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.5.2: Method B Construction in Drum Rack","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 METHOD B HARDWARE KIT CONFIGURATION (175 BPM)\n==================================================================================================\nPAD NO.   VOICE NAME      DEVICE ENGINE         PROCESSING CHAIN\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPad 1     Club Kick       DS Kick (Synth)       Tune: 100 Hz, Click: 85%, Drive: +3 dB (Direct Out)\nPad 3     Power Snare     DS Snare + Sample     Tuned @ 210 Hz, Hard Clipped @ 0.0 dBFS\nPad 4     Ensemble Clap   DS Clap (M4L)         High-Pass @ 600 Hz, Delayed +8 ms\nPad 7     Punchy Hat      Drum Sampler (12.1)   High-Pass @ 6 kHz, Punch FX: 40% (Choke 1)\nPad 11    Open Hat        Drum Sampler (12.1)   Decay: 350 ms, Choke 1 (Offbeat Accent)\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-5-3","title":"9.5.3: 16–32 Bar Phrasing and DJ Scaffolding","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.5.3: 16–32 Bar Phrasing and DJ Scaffolding","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Club DJs mix tracks using 32-bar and 64-bar phrases. Structuring your track to this scaffolding is non-negotiable:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 DANCEFLOOR DNB 32-BAR PHRASE SCAFFOLDING\n==================================================================================================\n[ PHRASE 1: BARS 1–16 (DROP PART A) ]\n• Bars 1–8   : Core Kick/Snare foundation + Bassline Lead\n• Bars 9–16  : Layer in offbeat Open Hats and Clap layer (Energy Step-Up)\n\n[ PHRASE 2: BARS 17–32 (DROP PART B) ]\n• Bars 17–24 : Add continuous 16th shaker / ride cymbals; maximum velocity\n• Bars 25–31 : Peak energy drive\n• Bar 32     : COMPLETE 1-BAR DRUM DROP (Silence / Snare Roll) -&gt; DJ Mix Out Marker\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-5-4","title":"9.5.4: Fills that a Club DJ Can Hear Coming","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.5.4: Fills that a Club DJ Can Hear Coming","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A club DJ spinning at 175 BPM in a loud venue cannot look at your waveform. They rely on <strong>binary auditory cues</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 THE 4-BEAT AUDITORY DJ CUE FILL (BAR 16 / BAR 32)\n==================================================================================================\nSTEP INDEX:     01    02    03    04    05    06    07    08    09    10    11    12    13    14    15    16\nMETRIC COUNT:   1     e     &amp;     a     2     e     &amp;     a     3     e     &amp;     a     4     e     &amp;     a\nKICK (Pad C1): [K]    .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .\nSNARE ROLL:     .     .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .    [S]   [S]   [S]   [S]   [S]   [S]   [S]   [S]\nVELOCITY:                               125                       85    95   105   115   120   124   127   127\nKICK ON 4:      .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .    [KICK ON EVERY 16TH!]\n==================================================================================================</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Bar 16 / Bar 32 Turnaround Rule:</strong> On Beat 3 and Beat 4 of the final measure, mute all background hi-hats and execute an ascending 16th-note snare roll that cuts to complete silence on Step 16. This provides an unambiguous signal to the DJ that the phrase has ended."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-5-5","title":"9.5.5: The 16-Bar Jump-Up Sketch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.5.5: The 16-Bar Jump-Up Sketch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Jump-Up, the snare is loud, dry, and aggressive:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Snare Peak:</strong> Placed 1.5–2.0 dB higher in the mix than in Liquid or Deep DnB.","<strong>Processing:</strong> <strong>Drum Buss</strong> with Hard Drive (25%) and Crunch (35%).","<strong>Bus Comp:</strong> Fast SSL-style Glue compression clamped to -4 dB gain reduction."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 9.5: Dancefloor Impact Workshop","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Ableton Live 12 to <code>175.00 BPM</code>.","Build the <strong>Method B Kit</strong> (DS Kick @ 100 Hz, DS Snare @ 210 Hz, Drum Sampler Hats).","Program a 32-bar Dancefloor drop:","Bars 1–16: Kick 1 &amp; 11, Snare 5 &amp; 13, closed 16th hats.","Bars 17–31: Add offbeat Open Hats (Steps 3, 7, 11, 15) and Ride cymbals.","Bar 32: Program the 4-Beat Auditory DJ Cue Fill.","Insert <strong>Drum Buss</strong> (Hard Drive: 20%, Transients: +0.25) on the drum bus.","Verify that the kick and snare punch through with massive physical impact."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>What is the primary technical distinction between Method B drums (Dancefloor/Jump-Up) and Method A drums (Jungle)?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Method B uses discrete, hard-clipped synthetic one-shot samples placed on rigid grid coordinates for maximum punch; Method A uses sampled, time-stretched acoustic funk breakbeats.","<strong>Why must Dancefloor Drum &amp; Bass strictly adhere to 16-bar and 32-bar phrasing?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> To align with standard club DJ mixing conventions and track structures, allowing DJs to execute seamless double-drops without phrase trainwrecking.","<strong>At what frequency should a high-impact Dancefloor/Jump-Up kick drum fundamental sit?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Between <strong>95 Hz and 110 Hz</strong> (sitting safely above the 45 Hz sub-bassline)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m9-6","code":"9.6","title":"Drum & Bass Engineering — Neurofunk, Techstep & Darkstep Precision","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-9-6","title":"Section 9.6 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Neurofunk</strong>, <strong>Techstep</strong>, and <strong>Darkstep</strong> represent the technological frontier of Drum &amp; Bass production. Originating in the late 1990s (Ed Rush &amp; Optical, Bad Company UK, Noisia, Black Sun Empire), these subgenres abandon acoustic nostalgia in favor of <strong>clinical transient precision</strong>, aggressive multi-band distortion, gated envelope decays, and cold industrial aesthetics. This module details the advanced DSP signal chains, transient gating, and micro-ghost editing required for high-velocity neuro drum production."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Engineer</strong> ultra-tight, clinical drum hits with zero pre-attack latency and truncated decay tails (&lt;60 ms).","<strong>Program</strong> cold, mechanical Techstep rhythms featuring industrial rimshots and metallic clangs.","<strong>Apply</strong> multi-stage saturation and aggressive transient shaping without destroying dynamic crest factor.","<strong>Deploy</strong> surgical ghost notes as mechanical precision markers rather than loose funk shuffles.","<strong>Construct</strong> an authentic 16-bar Neurofunk/Techstep drum sketch at 174.00 BPM."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-6-1","title":"9.6.1: Tight, Clinical Transient Engineering","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.6.1: Tight, Clinical Transient Engineering","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Neurofunk, drum transients must sound hyper-engineered, metallic, and razor-sharp:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  NEUROFUNK DRUM ACOUSTIC CRITERIA                       |\n|                                                                         |\n|  • KICK DRUM:                                                           |\n|    - Ultra-short decay (55 ms - 80 ms)                                  |\n|    - Hard-clipped transient click @ 5.0 kHz                             |\n|    - Aggressively high-passed @ 35 Hz to strip sub-rumble               |\n|                                                                         |\n|  • SNARE DRUM:                                                          |\n|    - Tight 200 Hz body + synthesized FM clang transient                 |\n|    - Gated decay: 90 ms (Total silence between hits)                    |\n|    - Processed through Overdrive &amp; Saturator (Hard Curve)               |\n|                                                                         |\n|  • HI-HATS:                                                             |\n|    - Synthesized DS HH (Mode: Synth/909) or Operator FM ticks           |\n|    - Gated to 25 ms; zero room reverb or acoustic bleed                 |\n+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-6-2","title":"9.6.2: Sparse Mechanical Techstep","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.6.2: Sparse Mechanical Techstep","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pioneered in the mid-1990s by Doc Scott and Trace, <strong>Techstep</strong> strips the drum track down to its bare industrial skeleton:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                           THE TECHSTEP 16-STEP MATRIX (174 BPM)\n==================================================================================================\nSTEP INDEX:     01    02    03    04    05    06    07    08    09    10    11    12    13    14    15    16\nMETRIC COUNT:   1     e     &amp;     a     2     e     &amp;     a     3     e     &amp;     a     4     e     &amp;     a\nKICK (Gated):  [K]    .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .     .    [K]    .     .     .     .     .\nTECH SNARE:     .     .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .     .     .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .\nMETAL RIM:      .     .     .    [R]    .     .     .     .     .    [R]    .     .     .     .    [R]    .\nCLOSED TICK:    .     .    [H]    .     .     .    [H]    .     .     .    [H]    .     .     .    [H]    .\n==================================================================================================</pre>"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Aesthetic:</strong> Cold, sparse, and robotic. There is zero acoustic warmth; hits are dry, clinical, and separated by black voids of silence."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-6-3","title":"9.6.3: Darkstep Aggression Without Grid Saturation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.6.3: Darkstep Aggression Without Grid Saturation","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A critical mistake in <strong>Darkstep</strong> production is attempting to create aggression by filling every 16th note with distorted hits."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Golden Rule:</strong> Aggression is created by <strong>timbral violence</strong>, not percussive density.","Keep the kick and snare pattern sparse (1+11 kick, 5+13 snare), but process each individual hit through heavy waveshaping, diode clipping, and comb filtering."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-6-4","title":"9.6.4: Ghost Notes as Mechanical Precision","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.6.4: Ghost Notes as Mechanical Precision","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Liquid or Jungle, ghost notes provide loose funk swing. In <strong>Neurofunk, ghost notes provide mechanical precision</strong>:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 NEUROFUNK GHOST NOTE MATRIX (SURGICAL ACCENTS)\n==================================================================================================\nSTEP INDEX:     01    02    03    04    05    06    07    08    09    10    11    12    13    14    15    16\nMETRIC COUNT:   1     e     &amp;     a     2     e     &amp;     a     3     e     &amp;     a     4     e     &amp;     a\nMAIN SNARE:     .     .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .     .     .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .\nGHOST TICK:     .     .     .    [G]    .     .     .    [G]    .    [G]    .     .     .    [G]    .     .\nVELOCITY:                        45    125               48          52               125   40\nENVELOPE:       All ghost hits gated to exactly 30 ms; pitch-tuned to track key center!\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-6-5","title":"9.6.5: The 16-Bar Neurofunk Drum Sketch","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.6.5: The 16-Bar Neurofunk Drum Sketch","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 16-BAR NEUROFUNK DRUM SKETCH PHRASING\n==================================================================================================\nBARS 1–4:   Kick + Snare + 16th Ticks (Razor-tight clinical foundation)\nBARS 5–8:   Add Operator FM metallic clangs on Steps 4, 10, 16\nBARS 9–12:  Introduce aggressive 32nd-note ratchet fills on Bar 10 &amp; 12\nBARS 13–15: Full dynamic intensity; multi-band saturation active on drum bus\nBAR 16:     Complete 1-bar drum drop; silence preceding the next Reese bass entry\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 9.6: Neurofunk Transient Engineering Workshop","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Set Ableton Live 12 to <code>174.00 BPM</code>.","Build a <strong>Neurofunk Drum Kit</strong>:","Kick: <strong>DS Kick</strong> (Tune: 105 Hz, Decay: 65 ms, Click: 90%).","Snare: <strong>DS Snare</strong> layered with <strong>Operator FM Click</strong> (Gated to 85 ms).","Hats: <strong>DS HH</strong> in Synth Mode (Decay: 25 ms).","Insert the <strong>Neuro Drum Bus Chain</strong>:","<strong>Utility</strong> (Bass Mono @ 120 Hz).","<strong>Overdrive</strong> (Drive: 15%, Tone: 6.5 kHz, Dynamics: 0.8).","<strong>Drum Buss</strong> (Hard Drive: 30%, Crunch: 40%, Transients: +0.35).","<strong>Glue Compressor</strong> (Hard Clip: ON, Ratio: 4:1, Attack: 30 ms).","Program the 16-bar Neuro sketch and verify the clinical, bone-dry impact."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>What acoustic envelope characteristic distinguishes a Neurofunk snare from a Liquid Funk snare?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> A Neurofunk snare is ultra-tight and gated (decay &lt;90 ms) with heavy digital saturation and zero acoustic room bleed; a Liquid snare has a natural acoustic body and decaying wire resonance (150–300 ms).","<strong>In Techstep drum programming, what role do ghost notes play compared to classic funk breakbeats?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> In Techstep, ghost notes serve as rigid, robotic mechanical markers rather than humanized, swung funk shuffles.","<strong>What is the optimal kick drum decay time range for a high-speed Neurofunk production?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>55–80 ms</strong>."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m9-7","code":"9.7","title":"Drum & Bass Engineering — Drumfunk, Atmospheric & 174 Halftime","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes This concluding Drum & Bass module explores the outer structural boundaries of the genre: Drumfunk (pure acoustic breakbeat virtuosity), Atmospheric / Intelligent DnB (wide ambient soundscapes anchored b","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-9-7","title":"Section 9.7 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This concluding Drum &amp; Bass module explores the outer structural boundaries of the genre: <strong>Drumfunk</strong> (pure acoustic breakbeat virtuosity), <strong>Atmospheric / Intelligent DnB</strong> (wide ambient soundscapes anchored by delicate breaks), <strong>Jazzstep</strong> (acoustic swing), and <strong>174 Halftime</strong> (slow-motion 87 BPM half-time bounce inside a 174 BPM master project)."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Deconstruct</strong> the virtuoso breakbeat arrangement philosophy of Drumfunk (Paradox, Seba).","<strong>Engineer</strong> wide, reverbed high-frequency tops for Atmospheric Drum &amp; Bass while preserving center kick/snare punch.","<strong>Construct</strong> Jazzstep breakbeat grooves featuring ghosted ride cymbals and brushed snares.","<strong>Program</strong> authentic 174 BPM Halftime rhythm arrangements.","<strong>Acknowledge</strong> historical boundary genres (Sambass, Drill 'n' Bass) with academic precision.","<strong>Pass</strong> Gate Assessment F."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-7-1","title":"9.7.1: Drumfunk: The Break as Virtuoso Composition","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.7.1: Drumfunk: The Break as Virtuoso Composition","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Drumfunk</strong> (pioneered by Paradox, Seba, Fanu, Equinox) is the purist discipline of Drum &amp; Bass."},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Philosophy:</strong> Synths and basslines are reduced to minimal, sub-bass drones. The entire musical and emotional narrative is carried by <strong>un-quantized, surgically chopped acoustic funk breaks</strong>.","<strong>Arrangement:</strong> Patterns evolve across 8, 16, and 32 bars with zero repetitive looping. Drummers' micro-dynamics, ghost strokes, and cymbal ringing are preserved with extreme fidelity."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-7-2","title":"9.7.2: Ragga and Sound System Bounce","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.7.2: Ragga and Sound System Bounce","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Metric Placement:</strong> Syncopated offbeat kicks (Steps 4, 7, 10) combined with pitched acoustic rimshots.","<strong>Dub Echo Automation:</strong> Automated auxiliary tape delay sends on the snare preceding major drops."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-7-3","title":"9.7.3: Jazzstep / Intelligent DnB: Acoustic Break Chops","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.7.3: Jazzstep / Intelligent DnB: Acoustic Break Chops","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pioneered in the mid-1990s by LTJ Bukem (Good Looking Records) and Roni Size / Reprazent:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Sound Palette:</strong> Multi-sampled acoustic ride cymbals, brushed snares, and upright acoustic bass samples.","<strong>Acoustic Feel:</strong> Smooth, rolling, un-clipped dynamics that complement live jazz saxophone, flute, and Rhodes chord voicings."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-7-4","title":"9.7.4: Atmospheric Drum & Bass: Spatial Staging","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.7.4: Atmospheric Drum & Bass: Spatial Staging","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"In Atmospheric DnB, the high-frequency tops are immersed in massive acoustic space:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ Drum Rack: Ride / Shaker Tops ] ---&gt; [ Hybrid Reverb: Shimmer / Space Hall ]\n                                            |\n                                            +--&gt; Decay: 3.5 seconds\n                                            +--&gt; Stereo Width: 140%\n                                            +--&gt; Low-Cut: High-pass @ 600 Hz</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Rule:</em> The kick drum fundamental and main 200 Hz snare body remain 100% dry and mono; only the ride cymbals, shakers, and ghost notes enter the reverb engine."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-7-5","title":"9.7.5: 174 Halftime: Heavy Half-Time Inside a 174 Set","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.7.5: 174 Halftime: Heavy Half-Time Inside a 174 Set","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 174 HALFTIME vs. FULL-TIME DNB (SAME 174.00 BPM CLOCK)\n==================================================================================================\nSTEP INDEX:     01    02    03    04    05    06    07    08    09    10    11    12    13    14    15    16\nMETRIC COUNT:   1     e     &amp;     a     2     e     &amp;     a     3     e     &amp;     a     4     e     &amp;     a\nFULL-TIME DNB: [K]    .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .     .     .    [K]    .    [S]    .     .     .\n174 HALFTIME:  [K]    .     .     .     .     .     .     .    [S]    .     .     .     .     .     .     .\nPERCEIVED FEEL: 87.00 BPM Heavy Head-Nod Pulse; Instant Energy Contrast!\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l9-7-6","title":"9.7.6: Academic Nomenclature: Sambass & Drill 'n' Bass","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"9.7.6: Academic Nomenclature: Sambass & Drill 'n' Bass","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Sambass (Brazilian DnB):</strong> Infuses Drum &amp; Bass at 170–174 BPM with traditional Brazilian samba polyrhythms (surdo, pandeiro, cuíca) and Latin jazz harmony (DJ Marky, Bungle).","<strong>Drill 'n' Bass / Breakcore:</strong> High-speed experimental subgenres (180–220 BPM) featuring algorithmically glitching, hyper-complex Amen break edits (Squarepusher, Aphex Twin). <em>Note: Named for historical taxonomy; detailed production recipes are beyond core club scope.</em>"],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 9.7: Gate F Capstone Construction","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Create a 174 BPM Ableton Live 12 Set.","Construct an authentic 16-bar Drum &amp; Bass drum arrangement:","<strong>Option A (Break-Based / Liquid / Jungle):</strong> Sliced Amen or Think break layered over clean synthetic kick and snare.","<strong>Option B (Kit-First Dancefloor / Neuro):</strong> Method B synthetic one-shots with tight 16th-note ghost precision and 32nd-note ratchet fills.","Apply the complete <strong>Master Drum Bus Chain</strong> (Utility Mono @ 120 Hz, EQ Eight, Drum Buss, Glue Compressor).","Save the clip and export an unmastered 24-bit WAV file."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Gate Assessment F Checklist (drums-F)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To clear Gate F, verify the following self-assessment criteria:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] An 8-to-16 bar drum arrangement at 174.00–175.00 BPM exists.","[ ] Pattern demonstrates either a sliced acoustic break (Method A) or a punchy kit-first architecture (Method B).","[ ] Snare strikes cleanly on Steps 5 and 13 (or Step 9 for Halftime).","[ ] Subgenre lane is explicitly named (e.g., Jungle, Liquid, Jump-Up, Neurofunk).","[ ] Drum bus peaks cleanly at ≈ -6.0 dBFS with active mono low-end summing."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>What is the defining compositional philosophy of Drumfunk?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Treating un-quantized, intricately sliced acoustic funk breakbeats as the primary melodic and rhythmic lead instrument rather than relying on synthesizers.","<strong>What is the perceived tempo when a snare is placed on Step 9 (Beat 3) in a 174 BPM DnB project?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>87.00 BPM</strong> (174 Halftime).","<strong>In Atmospheric Drum &amp; Bass, which drum layers are routed to large auxiliary reverb sends, and which layers remain strictly dry?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> High-frequency tops (rides, shakers, acoustic ghost clicks) are routed to wide auxiliary reverbs; the low-frequency kick fundamental and primary snare body remain strictly dry and mono-centered."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":20}],"exercises":[]}]};
